## What it does

`@rat-stack/motion` moves values over time in a Foldkit app. Its core is four TypeScript modules in `packages/motion/src/*.pure.ts`. They import only each other, so another project can copy them at a pinned commit.

- **Easing**: Robert Penner's ten curve families come in three variants each: in, out and in-out.
- **CSS easing**: `linear`, `cubic-bezier()`, `steps()` and keywords such as `ease-in-out` follow the CSS spec.
- **Tween**: a value moves between two endpoints over a duration along one easing. The `sample` function reads the value at any time. A retarget starts a new tween from the sampled value, so the value never jumps.
- **Spring**: a value of any dimension follows a target with stiffness, damping and mass. The `critical(frequency)` preset arrives without overshoot.
- **Interpolation**: numbers, tuples, colours in OKLab, and 2D transforms split into translate, scale and rotate.

The spring takes fixed steps of 1/120 s. It carries the leftover time to the next frame. When it comes to rest, it snaps exactly onto the target.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/motion/src/spring.pure.ts at=5fa5df1543b8122a678722d5465ae65fc8a4bf2d lines=245-259 {252-254} title="Each substep checks for rest, so the snap happens at the same instant at any frame rate"

```

The Foldkit [adapter](/lore/hexagonal-architecture), `@rat-stack/motion/foldkit`, keeps a clock and the named tweens and springs in the Model. A frame is a `TickedFrame` Message. `Motion.advance` steps every spring and moves the clock. The view samples each tween at the clock time.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/motion/src/foldkit.ts at=5fa5df1543b8122a678722d5465ae65fc8a4bf2d lines=186-202 title="Frames run only while something moves, and the reduced-motion preference arrives as a Message"

```

Pick a target in the demo at the end of this page, then pick another before the dots arrive. The tween restarts its curve. The spring keeps its speed and turns.

The curve gallery plots every easing.

## The standard

- Every easing starts at 0 and ends at 1. The CSS start jumps are the exception: `steps(n, jump-start)` begins at 1/n, as the spec requires.
- Every curve except back, elastic and bounce never falls. Every out curve mirrors its in curve: `out(t) = 1 - in(1 - t)`.
- `cubicBezier` agrees with an exact bisection reference for any valid control points.
- A tween with a monotonic easing stays between its endpoints. A retarget keeps the sampled value.
- One spring step of a span gives the same state as two steps that split it on the substep grid.
- A critically damped spring from rest settles within six periods, snaps exactly, and never overshoots in one dimension.
- Under `prefers-reduced-motion`, every tween and spring lands on its target at once.
- No Worker graph imports the package, so it adds no startup bytes.

Each rule is an `it.prop` property over generated inputs. Each property also runs against a planted bug, and the suite fails if the property passes it.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/motion/test/spring.test.ts at=5fa5df1543b8122a678722d5465ae65fc8a4bf2d lines=344-355 title="The frame-rate property must reject an integrator that takes one step per frame"

```

## How to check

- `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/motion test` runs the properties and the planted bugs.
- `packages/motion/test/vendoring.test.ts` fails if a core module imports anything except another core module, or names a browser global.
- `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/site typecheck` prints the Mischief startup size against its ceiling.
- Open [/systems/motion](/systems/motion) with reduced motion turned on. The dots and curves show their end state.
